\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{frap}
\title{Positions, orientations, and global constraints}

\p{A moved corner has both a destination and an orientation. The
[facet construction](frap-0009) transports its incident labels together.
To write that transport as a number or permutation, we need a local frame
at each position. The following finite model makes those choices explicit.
It is the wreath-product mechanism used in Marchetti's coordinate description
\citet{Section 3.1}{marchetti2025rubik}.}

\p{Let #{I} be a finite set of positions. At each position #{i}, let #{X_i}
be the same kind of local label set, with a chosen frame
#{c_i:\Omega\to X_i}. Let #{K} be a group acting faithfully on #{\Omega}.
We consider exactly those transports that send each whole fiber #{X_i}
to a fiber #{X_{\pi(i)}} and have a framed local map in #{K}. This is a
hypothesis on the model, not a conclusion for every puzzle encoding.}

\subtree{
  \taxon{Lemma}
  \title{The composition law}
  \p{Write a transport as #{g=(\pi,(a_i)_{i\in I})}, with action
  #{(i,x)\mapsto(\pi(i),a_i x)} in framed coordinates. If
  #{h=(\sigma,(b_i))}, applying #{g} and then #{h} gives}
  ##{hg=(\sigma\pi,(b_{\pi(i)}a_i)_{i\in I}).}
  \proof{
    \p{The intermediate coordinates are #{(\pi(i),a_i x)}. Applying
    #{h} evaluates its local map at that intermediate position, giving
    #{(\sigma\pi(i),b_{\pi(i)}a_i x)}. This also shows why a local
    factor cannot be multiplied without tracking its position index.}
  }
}

\p{All such framed transports form the wreath product
#{K\wr\operatorname{Sym}(I)}: the normal subgroup #{K^I} changes local
labels without moving positions, while permutations reorder the factors.
Our formula uses source-indexed factors and left actions. The notation
#{(\pi,a)} records this convention; formulas written with destination-indexed
factors look different. Legal puzzle moves usually generate a proper subgroup
of this ambient coordinate group.}

\subtree{
  \taxon{Theorem}
  \title{The abelianized total orientation}
  \p{Let #{K_{\mathrm{ab}}=K/[K,K]}, where #{[K,K]} is generated by
  commutators #{uvu^{-1}v^{-1}}. Write #{[a]} for the class of #{a}.
  The map}
  ##{\chi(\pi,a)=\prod_{i\in I}[a_i]\quad\hbox{in }K_{\mathrm{ab}}}
  \p{is a homomorphism. It is unchanged when the local frames are replaced
  by #{c_i'=c_i t_i}, with #{t_i\in K}.}
  \proof{
    \p{The composition law and commutativity of the target give}
    ##{\chi(hg)=\prod_i[b_{\pi(i)}][a_i]
      =\left(\prod_j[b_j]\right)\left(\prod_i[a_i]\right)
      =\chi(h)\chi(g).}
    \p{Here the middle equality reindexes the product using the bijection
    #{\pi}. In the new frames, the local map is
    #{a_i'=t_{\pi(i)}^{-1}a_i t_i}. Its abelianized product contains each
    #{[t_i]} once and each inverse once, so these cancel. The remaining
    product is #{\chi(g)}. This assertion concerns changes within the
    chosen group of frames; reversing a common orientation convention can
    also change the identification of the target group.}
  }
}

\p{The nonabelian product need not be independent of order or frames.
Abelianization is precisely what makes the reindexing and cancellation legal.
Nor is a character automatically conserved by the puzzle: conservation means
that every allowed generator lies in its kernel.}

\subtree{
  \taxon{Lemma}
  \title{Checking a generator cycle by cycle}
  \p{On a cycle #{i_0\mapsto i_1\mapsto\cdots\mapsto i_{r-1}\mapsto i_0}
  of a transport, the return map in the first fiber is
  #{a_{i_{r-1}}\cdots a_{i_1}a_{i_0}}. Its class is that cycle's
  contribution to #{\chi}. Consequently, identity return transport on
  every position cycle implies #{\chi=1}.}
  \proof{
    \p{Apply the coordinate rule #{r} times. The resulting local product
    is the stated one. In the abelianization its factors may be reordered;
    multiplying over disjoint position cycles uses each factor exactly
    once. A fixed position is a cycle of length one and must also be checked.}
  }
}

\subtree{
  \taxon{Example}
  \title{Corner twists and edge flips on the cube}
  \p{Use the unmarked-center, fixed-frame sticker model of
  [one cube face turn](frap-0009). At a corner the three incident sticker
  directions have a cyclic order inherited from the ambient orientation.
  Choose cyclic frames at all eight positions. Rotations preserve this
  order, so the local group is #{C_3}; at each of the twelve edges a chosen
  order of its two stickers gives local group #{C_2}. Write their elements
  additively. The two characters are then}
  ##{\chi_c=\sum_{i=1}^{8}a_i\pmod3,\qquad
      \chi_e=\sum_{j=1}^{12}b_j\pmod2.}
  \p{A quarter-turn has one four-cycle of affected corners and one of
  affected edges. Four applications restore every sticker, so the return
  transport on each of these cycles is the identity. Every other corner
  and edge is fixed with identity local transport. The cycle lemma gives
  #{\chi_c=0} and #{\chi_e=0} for every face quarter-turn, hence for
  every word in these moves, including their inverses and half-turns.}
  \p{For a concrete cyclic frame, choose any frame at corner #{i_0} of
  the upper face and define the next three by transporting it with the
  shape rotation #{q}. Since #{q^4=1}, all four local factors of this
  turn become zero. This is a convenient frame for checking that one
  generator; the frame-independence theorem transfers the conclusion to
  any fixed choice of cyclic frames used for the other generators.}
  \p{A configuration twisting just one corner by #{1} has
  #{\chi_c=1} and is impossible under these moves. Twisting two corners
  by #{1} and #{-1} passes this test, but that calculation alone does not
  construct a legal sequence. Likewise, flipping exactly one edge fails
  the second test. Reachability needs additional arguments.}
}

\p{A labelled corner followed through successive moves makes the distinction
visible: its coordinate depends on the chosen frame, while the total class
survives changes of those frames. Transfer gives a group-theoretic form of
this cancellation; higher-dimensional local groups show how much information
abelianization can discard.}
